    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives the Persian fleet 1,207 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> at the outset, but some modern historians say no more than around 600 could have been launched into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and most favour 600 to 800 at Salamis itself.</li><li>In the cramped straits the great Persian numbers hindered the fleet, as ships struggled to manoeuvre and became disorganised, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> were drawn in partly by a message <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>.</li><li>After Salamis the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> was safe from conquest and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> were dealt a blow to their prestige; the following year the remaining Persian army lost at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and the fleet at Mycale.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> had a throne set up on the slopes of Mount Aigaleo, overlooking the straits, so that he could watch the battle and record the names of commanders who performed particularly well.</p></aside>
    </section>

