    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">491 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Darius sends emissaries asking for earth and water; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> puts the ambassadors on trial and executes them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> throws them down a well</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A heavily outnumbered Athenian army defeats the Persian force at Marathon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">482 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, under the guidance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, decides to build a massive fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">481 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> at Corinth forms a confederate alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> for three days and fight at Artemisium, then the Allied fleet withdraws to Salamis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> sends Sicinnus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> with a message that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> mean to flee, and the Persian fleet is sent to block the straits</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Greek fleet defeats the Persian fleet in the straits of Salamis</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> win at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and the Allied fleet at Mycale</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-salamis" data-v="fb30e65715" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Persians block the southern exit</b> The Allied fleet lay off Salamis, the Athenians on the left and the Spartans probably on the right. The Persian fleet was sent out to block the exit from the straits, and about 400 troops stood on Psyttaleia.</li><li><b>The Persians pivot into the straits</b> The Persians seem to have swung their fleet round off Cape Vavari, from a line across the exit to a line running north to south. The Phoenicians were on the right by Mount Aigaleo and the Ionians on the left.</li><li><b>The Allies back their ships away</b> As the Persians came on, cramped and disordered in the narrow water, the Allies seemed to back away as if in fear. Plutarch gave the reason as a better position and time to wait for the early morning wind.</li><li><b>One ship shoots forward to ram</b> While the Allies were backing water, a single ship shot forward to ram the nearest Persian vessel. The Athenians claimed it as the ship of Ameinias of Pallene, and the Aeginetans as one of theirs.</li><li><b>The whole Greek line follows</b> The whole Greek line followed the first ship and made straight for the Persian battle line, which had become disorganised and cramped in the narrow water.</li><li><b>The Phoenicians are pushed back and the centre is split</b> The first Persian line fouled the second and third behind it. The leaderless Phoenician squadrons were pushed back against the coast, many running aground, and in the centre a wedge of Greek ships split the Persian fleet in two.</li><li><b>The Persian fleet begins to retreat</b> The Persian fleet began to retreat towards Phalerum, the centre and the Ionians turning back towards the exit from the straits.</li><li><b>The Aeginetans ambush the retreating ships</b> According to Herodotus the Aeginetans ambushed the Persians as they tried to leave the straits. The remaining Persian ships limped back to the harbour of Phalerum and the shelter of the Persian army.</li><li><b>Aristides destroys the garrison on Psyttaleia</b> After the Persian fleet had limped back, the Athenian general Aristides took a detachment across to Psyttaleia to slaughter the garrison that Xerxes had left there.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria had supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> against the Persian Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> in 499 to 494 BCE, and Darius vowed to punish those involved. In 491 BCE he sent emissaries to the Greek cities asking for earth and water as a token of submission. Most obliged, but in Athens the ambassadors were put on trial and executed, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> they were thrown down a well. In 490 BCE a force under Datis and Artaphernes destroyed Eretria and landed at Marathon, where a heavily outnumbered Athenian army won and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> withdrew to Asia.</p><p>Darius began raising a huge new army, but an Egyptian revolt in 486 BCE postponed it, and he died while preparing to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> crushed the revolt and restarted the preparations, which included bridging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and digging a canal across the isthmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. In Athens the decision of 482 BCE, under the guidance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, was to build a massive fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>. A congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> met at Corinth in late autumn 481 BCE and formed a confederate alliance, remarkable in a Greek world where many members were still technically at war with each other.</p><p>The allies first meant to hold the Vale of Tempe but withdrew, then adopted a dual strategy: the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> would be blocked on land and the straits of Artemisium by sea. The Spartan king Leonidas held Thermopylae for three days before being outflanked by a mountain path, and the Spartans and Thespians who stayed were surrounded and killed. The fighting at Artemisium had been a stalemate, and the Allied fleet withdrew when news of Thermopylae arrived.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports 378 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> in the Allied fleet, but his figures for the contingents add up to 371. The difference may be a garrison of 12 ships left at Aegina, and he says two ships defected from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, so the total at Salamis would be 373 or 380. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, who fought at Salamis, gives 310 triremes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> claims the Athenians had only 110, which ties in with Aeschylus, and Hyperides gives 220 for the whole fleet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> effectively commanded, but the Spartan nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurybiades">Eurybiades</a> was nominal leader, a compromise after the other states with navies objected to Themistocles claiming leadership.</p><p>For the Persians, Herodotus gives 1,207 triremes at the outset. By his reckoning about a third were lost in a storm off Magnesia, 200 more off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and at least 50 at Artemisium; he says the losses were replaced but names only 120 ships from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and nearby islands. Aeschylus also gives 1,207, Diodorus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysias">Lysias</a> 1,200 at Doriskos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a> 1,300 at Doriskos and 1,200 at Salamis, and Ctesias 1,000. Some modern historians accept 1,207 for the initial fleet, while others say the Persians could have launched no more than around 600 warships. Very few accept that many at Salamis: most favour 600 to 800.</p><p>Herodotus describes the Persian ships as having better sailing, probably owing to superior seamanship, while most Athenian ships were newly built and had inexperienced crews. He suggests the Allied ships were heavier, perhaps because of fully armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> marines. The Persians and Asiatic Greeks used a manoeuvre called the diekplous, probably rowing into gaps between ships and ramming them in the side, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> developed tactics to counter it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeschylus.jpg" alt="Aeschylus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herma_of_Aeschylus,_Klas08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the Allied fleet the Athenians were on the left and probably the Spartans on the right, though Diodorus says it was the Megareans and Aeginetans, with the other contingents in the centre. The Persian fleet was sent to block the exit from the straits the evening before. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> believed it entered the straits at nightfall, but modern historians have debated this, some pointing to the difficulty of manoeuvring in a confined space by night. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a> gives the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> three ranks, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> on the right next to Mount Aigaleo and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> on the left. About 400 troops were placed on the island of Psyttaleia, and Diodorus adds that the Egyptian fleet was sent round Salamis to block the northern exit, which Herodotus does not mention.</p><p>The Persians did not attack until daylight. Aeschylus says that as they approached they heard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> singing their battle hymn. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> at first appeared to back their ships away, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says was to gain better position and to wait for the early morning wind. Herodotus recounts that a single ship then shot forward to ram the nearest Persian vessel; the Athenians claimed it was the ship of Ameinias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallene">Pallene</a>, the Aeginetans one of theirs. The whole Greek line followed.</p><p>As the first Persian line was pushed back it fouled the second and third lines. On the Greek left the Persian admiral Ariabignes, a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>, was killed early, and the leaderless Phoenician squadrons appear to have been pushed against the coast, many vessels running aground. In the centre a wedge of Greek ships split the Persian fleet in two. The Persians began to retreat towards Phalerum, and Herodotus says the Aeginetans ambushed them as they tried to leave the straits.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darafsh, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> does not give the Persian losses, but he writes that the next year the Persian fleet numbered 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>. The number lost therefore depends on the size of the fleet at the start; something in the range of 200 to 300 seems likely on the estimates above. Herodotus says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> suffered many more casualties than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> because most of them did not know how to swim. The Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> took a detachment across to Psyttaleia and killed the garrison <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> had left there. Herodotus says Xerxes had Phoenician captains beheaded for blaming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> for cowardice; Diodorus says he put to death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> chiefly responsible for the flight, and that the Phoenicians sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> that night.</p><p>Xerxes tried to build a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridge</a> or causeway across the straits to attack the Athenians, but with the Greek fleet patrolling the straits it proved futile. At a council of war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>, according to Herodotus, offered to attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> or, if the king was resolved to withdraw, to remain and conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> with 300,000 chosen troops. Fearing that the Greeks might attack the bridges over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and trap his army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, Xerxes took the greater part of the army home. Mardonius picked the troops who stayed, the elite infantry and cavalry. The Persians left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, Mardonius wintered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, and the Athenians returned to their burnt city.</p><p>In 479 BCE Mardonius recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> under Spartan leadership marched on Attica. The two armies met near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, where the Greeks won, destroying much of the Persian army. At the near-simultaneous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Battle of Mycale</a> the Allied fleet destroyed much of the remaining Persian fleet.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is not well described by ancient sources, and it is unlikely that anyone other than perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> had a clear idea what was happening across the width of the straits. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> is the main narrator, and the playwright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, who fought at Salamis, also gives details and figures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, Diodorus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> and Hyperides supply further claims, and they often disagree. What can be written is more of a discussion than a definitive account.</p><p>The timeline is difficult to establish. Herodotus presents the battle as though it occurred directly after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> but nowhere says so explicitly, and it is more likely that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> spent two or three weeks capturing Athens, refitting and resupplying. It is also hard to explain exactly what brought about the battle. The rift among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> that reached Xerxes may have been a ruse, or a response to Persian offensive manoeuvres, and the Peloponnesians may have been party to the stratagem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, so calmly did they accept that they must fight. Whether the Persians entered the straits at night or at dawn is debated. The Athenian story that the Corinthians sailed away at the start was denied by all the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and the Athenians and Aeginetans each claimed the first ramming. The Persian losses and the size of both fleets depend on figures that the sources do not agree on.</p><p>On 17 March 2017 archaeologists announced that they had uncovered the partially submerged remains of the anchorage used by the Greek warships before the battle, at the Ambelaki-Kynosaurus site on the island of Salamis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea.jpg" alt="Battle of Plataea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scene_of_the_Battle_of_Plataea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Steeple Davis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Salamis marked the turning point of the Greco-Persian wars. After it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and by extension <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> as an entity, was safe from conquest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> suffered a major blow to their prestige and morale as well as severe material losses. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Mycale the threat of conquest was removed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> could go on the counter-offensive. The Greek victory allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> to revolt against Persian rule, and over the next 30 years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> were removed from Persian control by the Allies or by their Athenian-dominated successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>.</p><p>Like Marathon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, Salamis has gained something of a legendary status, perhaps because of the desperate circumstances and the unlikely odds. A significant number of historians have called it one of the most significant battles in human history. In a more extreme form of the argument, some historians hold that a Greek defeat would have stifled the growth of Western civilisation, on the premise that philosophy, science, personal freedom and democracy are rooted in the legacy of ancient Greece. The celebrated blossoming of Athenian culture came only after the Persian wars were won.</p><p>Militarily it is difficult to draw many lessons from Salamis, because of the uncertainty about what happened. The Allies chose their ground to negate Persian numbers but had to rely on the Persians launching an attack that, by the argument of Artemisia, they did not need to make; perhaps the most important lesson is the deception by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, the previously undistinguished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> won great repute among the Athenians for his courage in the battle, which later enabled him to launch his political career.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Salamis in 480 BCE an Allied Greek fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> fought a larger Persian fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> in the narrow straits off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and won, after which Xerxes withdrew with much of his army. The size of both fleets and the Persian losses are disputed.</em></p>

